  "id": "call-sheet",
  "title": "Call sheet: who to call and why",
  "subtitle": "The 30 builders with the strongest public reason for a land banker to call now (of 40 with at least one reason), highest score first. 29 have a named land or finance contact. Points are an estimate.",
  "is_estimate": true,
  "method": "Estimate. Each builder earns points for public reasons to call, added up: 30 if half or more of its lots are optioned and no land banker is publicly named (or 20 if it says it uses land banking but does not name the banker); 25 if any outstanding debt comes due by 2028-11-30 (same rules as the \"Debt coming due\" table); 20 for a builder acquisition since the list was published (May 2026), as buyer or as the company bought; 15 if the first line of its land strategy states a land-light or asset-light approach; 15 if a corporate land or finance executive took the seat in the last 12 months (on or after 2025-11-19); plus 10 for a top-25 builder or 5 for a top-50 builder. The top 30 are kept; ties go to the higher-ranked builder. The reason sentence lists the scoring facts, largest first, and the source column is the source of the largest. Person to call: a corporate land head first (someone whose title names land; acquisition before development), then treasury or capital markets, then the CFO, then any other corporate land-team executive, Verified before Reported before Inferred; if none is recorded the row says \"no land or finance contact found\" and no one else is substituted. Contact details are shown only when the company publishes them (pattern-built emails are left out). Named land bankers are read from the \"Who banks whom\" table (competitor research) so the names match it, falling back to the same name rules applied to the builder's own records if that table is missing; \"none named\" means none found in public sources, not that none exists. Left out: nonprofits (Habitat for Humanity International, NeighborWorks America) and build-to-rent operators and contractors (AMH, Middleburg Communities, BBL Building Co., Hancock Builders, Lifestyle Homes, Jim Chapman Construction Group). What is missing: the owned-versus-optioned split is public for only 21 builders and debt for 57, so private builders score low on thin data, not on low need; start dates are recorded for about a quarter of executives. The weights are a first guess, printed so they can be changed. Run 2026-11-19.",
